  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 129 AM EDT Tue Aug 11 2026\n\nQuasi-stationary boundary remains in nearly the same area as 24 \nhours ago, from near Davenport, IA east to Chicago and then curving \ntowards Detroit. Much like last night, an area of showers and storms \nhas exited the forecast area with attention turning upstream across \nE IA into N IL where CAMs develop additional convection over the \nnext several hours and track E or SE. As of 5Z a few cells have \npopped from near Gary NW to Poplar Grove IL. Intensification has \nbeen on the slower side, but LLJ is starting to increase with 25 to \n30 kts now noted on VADs in Iowa and central IL and mesoanalysis \nshows a pocket of over 2000 J/KG of MLCAPE. Pops have been adjusted \nsomewhat, but may need further changes later depending on evolution. \nA threat for large hail, locally gusty winds, frequent lightning and \nheavy rain would be the main concerns.\n\nTowards 12Z most of the activity looks to push away with several \nCAMs hinting at new development along the washed out boundary from W \nof Chicago to White county. As the low level flow increases from the \nSW, this looks to shift NE allowing a small window for rapid \ndestabilization. With MLCAPE over 2000 J/KG, DCAPE over 1000 J/KG \nand at least modest shear. A line of showers and storms is expected \nto develop upstream and dive quickly SE, arriving as early as 18Z in \nW/NW areas. Some concerns that the morning convection could impact \nthe overall strength of the line and possibly focus the greater wind \nthreat SW of us. Upper level flow, approaching wave and other \nfactors above all point towards the potential of a higher end wind \nevent along with a small chance of brief spin ups. New SWODY1 \nmaintains the Enhanced risk across the SW half of the area with a \nCIG1 hatching for the increased wind threat. Alignment of best pops \nmay change this morning as we see what, if any impacts occur from \novernight and early morning convection. \n\nIn addition to the severe threat, flooding concerns will remain on \nthe front burner as swaths of heavier rainfall have occurred across \nthe CWA over the past couple of days, most recently in SE area. \nGiven the lower confidence on the evolution of convection through \nearly evening was going to hold on any flood watch, but after \ncollaboration in house and surrounding offices decided to hoist a \nFlood Watch for all of our IN and OH counties with most of our MI \ncounties spared of the worst rainfall. \n\nThe line should quickly push out near/after 00Z Wed with the frontal \nboundary finally getting a nudge south into central IN. Does this \nmean that Wed and beyond will see much calmer conditions...probably \nnot as the entire upper level flow really doesn't change much the \nremainder of the week and troughs moving through the area will cause \nthe front to shift north and south. As a result, pops remain riddled \nin several period through the remainder of the forecast. Given the \nchallenges the next 12 to 24 hours, anything outside tonight was \nleft alone.",
